es add -C`.\n   - What I ran (scratch repo, 1,100-line commit): I planted a version with 500 groups each claiming the whole hunk. `show HEAD` refused it (\"the groups claim 550500 changed lines … at most 500000\"). I re-glossed with `add --force`, then ran `versions --restore 2`. It printed `restored version 2 as the gloss of 5161e5c` and exited 0, and `show HEAD` then failed with the same refusal.\n   - So a restore can still plant a gloss that breaks a single-commit `show` or `html`. Inside a range, the new skip-with-warning path limits the damage.\n\n2. **On the ary server, a refused gloss looks like an unglossed commit** (minor, **read**). `src/html/data.ts:328-330`, called from `src/ary/desk.ts:97`\n   - In a range, `buildPageData` only sends the refusal to `console.error`. The ary page is built in a child process, so that warning never reaches the person in the browser.\n   - Scenario: someone pushes a gloss of 501 groups (or anything over 8 MiB) to one commit of a branch. In ary, that commit appears as \"not glossed\", with its whole diff marked unexplained and no hint that a gloss exists and was refused. `show` at least prints \"gloss refused (see the warning)\"; the page carries no such marker. The `html` command has the same gap, but it does print the warning on stderr there.\n\nThe rest of the evidence comes from the orchestrator's runs, which I didn't repeat: tsc clean, `hardnotes.test.ts` passing, and the two `arysync` timing failures.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
